  <section class="pb-section" aria-labelledby="no-cap">
    <h2 id="no-cap">The Single Biggest Risk in Part B: No Annual Out-of-Pocket Cap</h2>

    <p>
      Most people assume Medicare covers everything after a small deductible. The reality of Original Medicare Part B is different: there is no annual limit on the 20% you owe. This is the most consequential fact about Part B cost structure, and it affects planning decisions significantly.
    </p>

    <div class="pb-scenario-table-wrap">
      <table class="pb-table">
        <thead>
          <tr>
            <th>Medical Service (Example)</th>
            <th>Total Billed Charge</th>
            <th>Your 20% with No Medigap</th>
            <th>Your Cost with Medigap Plan G</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr>
            <td>Outpatient surgery</td>
            <td>$25,000</td>
            <td class="pb-no" style="font-weight:700;">$5,000</td>
            <td class="pb-yes" style="font-weight:700;">$0 (after $283 deductible)</td>
          </tr>
          <tr class="pb-row-alt">
            <td>Chemotherapy course (typical)</td>
            <td>$60,000</td>
            <td class="pb-no" style="font-weight:700;">$12,000</td>
            <td class="pb-yes" style="font-weight:700;">$0 (after $283 deductible)</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>Major trauma / critical illness</td>
            <td>$200,000</td>
            <td class="pb-no" style="font-weight:700;">$40,000</td>
            <td class="pb-yes" style="font-weight:700;">$0 (after $283 deductible)</td>
          </tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>

    <p>
      These are illustrative examples based on typical Medicare-approved amounts — not guaranteed figures for any specific service. But they show why the no-cap rule matters. A Medigap (Medicare Supplement) plan covers the 20% coinsurance that Original Medicare leaves unpaid.
    </p>

